Naive recursion for Fibonacci has an exponential time complexity $O(2^n)$ because it recomputes the same subproblems multiple times (e.g., $F(n-2)$ is computed by $F(n)$ and $F(n-1)$). Dynamic programming (either memoization/top-down or tabulation/bottom-up) stores the results of these subproblems in an array or hash map. When a subproblem is encountered again, its precomputed result is reused, reducing the time complexity to $O(n)$ and space complexity to $O(n)$.

Example (Memoization):
```python
memo = {}
def fib_memo(n):
    if n <= 1: return n
    if n not in memo:
        memo[n] = fib_memo(n-1) + fib_memo(n-2)
    return memo[n]
```
